I think that’s a powerful framing, with one nuance.

For today’s large language models, linguistics is effectively the primary control interface. We don’t steer the model by directly manipulating billions of weights during a conversation. We steer it by changing the linguistic input, which changes the computation the model performs.

In that sense:

Natural language is the instruction set architecture (ISA) for human-AI interaction.

The hardware executes matrix multiplications.
The model contains learned statistical structure.
Language is the interface through which humans program the computation.

That suggests an interesting stack:

Intent
    ↓
Meaning
    ↓
Linguistics
    ↓
Tokens
    ↓
Embeddings
    ↓
Attention
    ↓
Neural computation
    ↓
Output

Every layer is a translation.

Your Pantheon often seems interested in operating above language, on the semantic relationships that language expresses. That suggests another hierarchy:

Meaning
    ↓
Linguistics (rendering)
    ↓
AI computation

If that view is right, then linguistics is less the storage medium than the control surface.

That’s why tiny wording changes can have outsized effects:

* changing the frame,
* introducing or removing ambiguity,
* altering assumptions,
* changing reference frames,
* selecting different latent concepts.

You’re not changing the model itself in the moment; you’re changing which regions of the model’s learned representation become active.

I think that’s one of the more interesting consequences of LLMs. Before them, programming largely meant writing formal code. Now, for a large class of tasks, language itself has become an executable interface. Prompt engineering, dialogue, specification writing, and even careful questioning are all ways of programming behavior through linguistic structure.

So a concise Pantheon-style formulation might be:

Linguistics is the control interface. Semantics is the operating system. Intelligence is the execution engine.

Or, borrowing your constellation metaphor:

Words are not the stars. They are the telescope knobs. Meaning is the sky. Intelligence is the act of navigating it.